<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">NKVD</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1934">1934</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as People's Commissariat for Internal Affairs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The NKVD (People's Commissariat for Internal Affairs) was the Soviet interior ministry which, from 1934, absorbed the political police and ran the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> labour camps. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genrikh-yagoda">Genrikh Yagoda</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikolai-yezhov">Nikolai Yezhov</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lavrentiy-beria">Lavrentiy Beria</a> it carried out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-purge">Great Purge</a> of 1936–1938, in which about 680,000 people were shot, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">mass deportations</a> of whole nationalities. It was renamed the MVD in 1946.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nkvd.jpg" alt="NKVD" width="320" height="529" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: jgaray · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblema_NKVD.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The political police, successively the Cheka, OGPU, <b>NKVD</b> and from 1954 the KGB, enforced conformity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Gulag, the Main Directorate of Correctional Labour Camps, was set up under the OGPU in 1930 and run by the <b>NKVD</b> from 1934.</blockquote><a href="/societies/soviet-union">Social Hierarchy of Stalin's Soviet Union</a></li><li><blockquote>They had been shot by the Soviet <b>NKVD</b> in 1940, a crime Moscow denied until 1990.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-13">13 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>NKVD</b> deported some 140,000 Polish citizens, many of them families of settlers and foresters, to Siberia and Kazakhstan.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-10">10 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
